etm-powersync-plugins (1.15.2+etm16) trixie; urgency=medium

  * openmeteo: the scalar states of a plane (forecastPvPower, forecastEnergy*)
    follow the decision of the run, as the curve does: corrected for module
    temperature together with forecastCurveTransport, or neither. Energies
    are sums of the same rounded hourly powers as p. forecastPvPower uses
    current.temperature_2m, now requested; when a corrected run lacks it, the
    state is not updated rather than published uncorrected.
  * The plane's forecast request sends the site's elevation, like the site's
    request. Measured on two replies of the same minute: GTI identical,
    tempC +0.1 K on 1 slot of 48, p unchanged. forecastCurveTransport keeps
    v 2, its fields and its computation, checked byte for byte against the
    etm15 code on four recorded replies.
  * A failed source is retried after 5 min x 2^(n-1), capped at its interval,
    +/-60 s of jitter, reset at the first success - for the forecast pass of
    a site and its planes (retried as a whole), the satellite of the site and
    of each plane, and the ensemble. It was asked again every minute. A 200
    reply that carries no data counts as a failure.
  * New plane state observedGtiSeries (v 1): the hourly satellite GTI of
    yesterday and today, slot i = [t0 + i*3600, t0 + (i+1)*3600), null when
    not observed. observedGti and observedGtiTime are unchanged.
  * New plane states forecastWeatherSpreadTime and forecastWeatherSpreadValid.
    Beyond 21600 s without a successful spread, the spread percentages become
    NaN (null over JSON-RPC), never 0, and Valid turns false.
  * New site state forecastHourlyWeather (v 1), for the app: WMO weather code,
    is_day and temperature_2m per hour for today and tomorrow, each value
    instantaneous at the start of its slot, null when absent. The three
    variables ride on the site's existing hourly request: no new request, no
    new host.
  * CONTRACT_A v4 and README describe all of the above.
  * Not deployed. Built for testing only; the keba package rebuilt from the
    same source is not to be published.

 -- Patrick Schurig <etm.schurig@gmail.com>  Thu, 15 Oct 2026 10:00:00 +0200

etm-powersync-plugins (1.15.2+etm15) trixie; urgency=medium

  * openmeteo: forecastCurveTransport v2. The hourly curve of each plane is
    corrected for module temperature (NOCT model, declared parameters, nothing
    learned): Tcell = T2m + GTI x (NOCT - 20) / 800, then
    p = max(0, GTI x kWp x PR x (1 + coeff/100 x (Tcell - 25))). No AC limit:
    clipping is a site parameter, applied by ems after the sum of the planes.
  * The correction applies to a whole run or not at all. Without a complete
    temperature series the run is published uncorrected, corrections = [],
    and p equals v1 bit for bit.
  * The payload keeps every v1 field and adds gti[48] (raw W/m2) and tempC[48]
    (temperature_2m, null where absent, never 0), for the record. corrections
    becomes a list, ["temperature"] or [], instead of the string "none"; v = 2.
  * New plane parameters tempCoeffPctPerK (-0.37 %/K, [-1, 0]) and noctC
    (45 C, [30, 70]); existing planes take the defaults. temperature_2m is added
    to the existing hourly request of the plane: no new host, no new data sent.
  * Deploy only after ems 1.15.2+etm66, which reads v1 and v2. An older ems
    refuses every v2 curve.
  * CONTRACT_A realigned on the code (state names, request, parameters, failure
    behaviour), then sec. 2.6 and 5.9 describe the correction and v2.

 -- ETM-Schurig SARL <contact@etm-schurig.eu>  Tue, 13 Oct 2026 10:00:00 +0200

etm-powersync-plugins (1.15.2+etm14) trixie; urgency=medium

  * openmeteo: a site now drives the hourly forecast of all its planes, in one
    pass, so every plane of a site is sampled at the same instant and carries the
    same run. Planes were gated independently — each with its own last-refresh
    time and its own re-rolled jitter — so two planes of one site drifted apart
    within minutes, and at each run publication one picked the new run up to an
    hour before the others. A consumer summing the planes of a site (the right
    thing to do, since the curve is compared against measured production, which is
    the roof total) could only block, or write a chimera: half the roof current,
    half stale, published as the whole.
  * CONTRACT_A sec. 5.7 states the guarantee; sec. 3.3 corrects the rule for
    forecastIssuedAt == 0 — store the age as NULL, never substitute an instant,
    but do not discard a perfectly usable curve for a missing label.

 -- ETM-Schurig SARL <contact@etm-schurig.eu>  Wed, 09 Sep 2026 08:20:00 +0200

etm-powersync-plugins (1.15.2+etm13) trixie; urgency=medium

  * openmeteo: the plugin timer now ticks every minute instead of every 15. With a
    900 s tick the +/-60 s jitter could only choose between the 60 min and the
    75 min tick — measured overnight in testing: 67 min mean instead of 60. The
    real defect was not the mean but that a whole fleet stayed aligned on the same
    quarter-hour grid, so the jitter de-correlated almost nothing, which is its
    only purpose. A jitter needs a resolution finer than the spread it produces.
  * CONTRACT_A sec. 3.1 now states the cadence that actually happens.

 -- ETM-Schurig SARL <contact@etm-schurig.eu>  Wed, 09 Sep 2026 06:30:00 +0200

etm-powersync-plugins (1.15.2+etm12) trixie; urgency=medium

  * RETRACTION. The 1.15.2+etm11 entry states that "a new run with the same
    numbers still differs by issuedAt and is published as a genuine new
    revision". That is wrong and is retracted here. The plugin deduplicates on
    the whole payload, issuedAt included, so such a run is indeed republished —
    but it is not a revision, the forecast having not changed. Deduplicating on
    the p values alone, and updating issuedAt on the existing row, belongs to the
    consumer. CONTRACT_A sec. 5.4 carries the corrected wording.
  * openmeteo: the forecast curve embedded a stale revision key. The plane read
    forecastIssuedAt from the site's state, which races the asynchronous
    meta.json round trip, so the curve carried the PREVIOUS run — seen in
    testing after a night, wrong by a whole AROME run (3 h). The curve is now
    built inside the provenance continuation and never reads the site's state. A
    revision keyed on the wrong run is precisely what sec. 3.3 exists to
    prevent.

 -- ETM-Schurig SARL <contact@etm-schurig.eu>  Wed, 09 Sep 2026 06:10:00 +0200

etm-powersync-plugins (1.15.2+etm11) trixie; urgency=medium

  * openmeteo: publish forecastCurveTransport on the PV plane — a QVariantMap
    carrying the 48-hour forecast power curve for ems to store. Typed and declared
    in the ThingClass rather than a JSON string, so there is no second encoding
    layer inside a declared state, and a probe reading it sees what it carries.
  * The window is whole days, today 00:00 local through tomorrow 23:00, elapsed
    hours included. Fixed day boundaries are what make two revisions of the same
    day comparable slot by slot; a rolling future-only window would shrink through
    the day and no two revisions would line up.
  * cached: false — a curve reloaded at startup would be stale and would misdate
    the first revision. Never logged, in the log engine or in qCDebug.
  * Published only when it changes: setStateValue discards an unchanged value, so
    an identical curve is neither stored nor notified. A new run with the same
    numbers still differs by issuedAt and is published as a genuine new revision.
  * model and issuedAt travel inside the payload, so a stored revision carries its
    own key with no second lookup.

 -- ETM-Schurig SARL <contact@etm-schurig.eu>  Tue, 08 Sep 2026 17:50:00 +0200

etm-powersync-plugins (1.15.2+etm10) trixie; urgency=medium

  * openmeteo: publish forecastIssuedAt (model run initialisation, the revision
    key) and forecastAvailableAt (publication, informational only). The gap
    between the two is 4 to 9 hours and varies by model — measured 2026-09-08 —
    so dating a revision on availability would attribute it to a run that did not
    yet exist, plausibly and undetectably.
  * openmeteo: meta.json is fetched with If-None-Match. It carries an ETag whose
    first field is the publication timestamp, so 304 with an empty body means the
    run has not changed, and the ETag itself is the new-run signal.
  * openmeteo: composite model names have no meta.json — meteofrance_seamless,
    best_match, icon_seamless all answer HTTP 500, they are blends and not grid
    domains. The model is translated to a provenance domain: seamless maps to
    meteofrance_arome_france_hd, whose run carries the first 60 hours; best_match
    maps to nothing, because it is resolved per location and has no single run
    identity.
  * openmeteo: when meta.json does not answer, both states go to 0 rather than to
    a substitute. A consumer must skip the revision; substituting the reception
    time would misdate it by hours. CONTRACT_A sec. 3.3.

 -- ETM-Schurig SARL <contact@etm-schurig.eu>  Tue, 08 Sep 2026 17:20:00 +0200

etm-powersync-plugins (1.15.2+etm8) trixie; urgency=medium

  * openmeteo: implement the model fallback of CONTRACT_A sec. 3.2, and make
    forecastModel a provenance rather than an intention. It published the
    configured model unconditionally, which was only ever correct because no
    fallback existed. A model that does not serve radiation answers HTTP 200
    with nulls, not an error — meteofrance_arome_france_hd serves none at all —
    so the reply is now inspected and best_match is used when nothing usable
    came back. forecastModel is set only from the request that actually served
    data, on the site and on the plane alike.

